<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sebastián Lerdo de Tejada</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1823">1823</a>–<a href="/years/1889">1889</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sebastián Lerdo de Tejada was a Mexican liberal lawyer and statesman who served as president from 1872 to 1876. A close minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-juarez">Benito Juárez</a> during the war against the French intervention, he succeeded him on his death and wrote the Reform laws into the constitution in 1873. His attempt to secure re-election provoked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porfirio-diaz">Porfirio Díaz</a>'s rebellion under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plan-of-tuxtepec">Plan of Tuxtepec</a>, and he died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sebastian-lerdo-de-tejada.jpg" alt="Sebastián Lerdo de Tejada" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francisco de Paula Mendoza · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Presente_Sebasti%C3%A1n_Lerdo_de_Tejada.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sebastián Lerdo de Tejada was born in Xalapa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a>, on 24 April 1823, into a middle-class Criollo family, the younger brother of Miguel Lerdo de Tejada. He studied theology on a scholarship at the Palafoxiano Seminary in Puebla but decided against the priesthood. He took a law degree at the Colegio de San Ildefonso in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> in 1851 and directed the college from 1852 to 1863. He became known as a Liberal leader, was foreign minister for three months in 1857 under Ignacio Comonfort, and presided over the Chamber of Deputies in 1861, 1862 and 1863.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>When the Republican government left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> on 31 May 1863, Lerdo de Tejada remained loyal to it. On 12 September 1863 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-luis-potosi">San Luis Potosí</a> he became Juárez's minister of foreign affairs and was the president's closest ally throughout the French occupation. He came third in the presidential election of 1871, behind Juárez and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porfirio-diaz">Porfirio Díaz</a>. Juárez died of a heart attack in July 1872, and Lerdo de Tejada, as president of the Supreme Court, was his constitutional successor and won election in his own right in November.</p><p>He kept Juárez's cabinet almost unchanged. He pacified the country, using federal troops to end the regional rule of Manuel Lozada of Tepic. In January 1873 he inaugurated the railway from Veracruz to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, begun under Juárez. The Laws of the Reform were incorporated into the constitution on 25 September 1873, and the Sisters of Charity were expelled from the country.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Lerdo de Tejada ran again in 1876, which gave opponents grounds under the principle of no re-election. Díaz issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plan-of-tuxtepec">Plan of Tuxtepec</a> before the election of 24 July 1876, which Lerdo won, though some charged fraud. Díaz defeated Lerdo's forces at Tecoac on 16 November 1876 and took the presidency on 28 November. Lerdo went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, where he died on 21 April 1889, and Díaz had his body returned for burial with full honours in the Rotonda of Illustrious Men. His overthrow marks the end of the Restored Republic and the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porfiriato">Porfiriato</a>. A statue of him stands outside the Mexican Congress, and Toluca de Lerdo bears his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sebastián Lerdo de Tejada” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the period known as the Restored Republic, Juárez and, after his death in 1872, <b>Sebastián Lerdo de Tejada</b> sought to enforce the Reform laws, build railways and curb the regional military chiefs within the constitutional order of 1857.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mexico">Mexico · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Xalapa, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. Died in New York City, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.53,-96.92&amp;year=1865" aria-label="Xalapa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1932 1440 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sebastián Lerdo de Tejada"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2382 1710h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2382 1710h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.53,-96.92&amp;year=1865">What was here in 1865? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
